  "answer": "Under the Act: section 43 requires rejection if a connotation of the trade mark, or a sign within it, makes its use for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion. It is not simply a test of whether customers might be misled for any reason. [TMA1995/s43]\n\nUnder Manual practice: the decision maker must be clearly satisfied that this test is met, and a ground for rejection should then be raised. The Manual states that there is no discretion to accept a mark caught by section 43. [TMM/Part29/1#1]\n\nRaising the ground does not necessarily end the application: the Manual says it can often be overcome by restricting the specification to goods or services for which the connotation is not deceptive or confusing. [TMM/Part20/5/5/5/2]\n\nWho must refuse it? The supplied passages do not expressly identify the office-holder responsible for formally rejecting the application. Section 43 says the application “must be rejected”, while the Manual refers to a “decision maker”; those passages alone do not establish a more specific answer. [TMA1995/s43; TMM/Part29/1#1]"
